+//! A fault proxy that lives inside the test: the failures you are afraid of, on demand.
+//!
+//! Every other fixture in this directory is a thing a *service* talks to. This one is a thing the
+//! test puts *between* the worker and its subscriber: it accepts a connection on a port of its own,
+//! reads the request, and either forwards it to the target or does something the target would never
+//! do — answer slowly, close the connection, never answer at all, drop it.
+//!
+//! ## Why this is not toxiproxy (or any other proxy container)
+//!
+//! The usual answer to "inject network faults" is a proxy sidecar: `toxiproxy`, Pumba, a `tc`
+//! netem. This course does not use one, and the reasons are the same three every time a decision
+//! like this comes up here (`docs/curriculum.md` §7 names toxiproxy in the tooling list, and this is
+//! a deliberate departure from it):
+//!
+//! * **it has to work offline.** The specs run on a laptop, in CI, and in a sandbox with no
+//!   registry. A proxy that has to be pulled is a spec that fails on the machine that has no network
+//!   — and the failure looks like a broken step, not like a missing image;
+//! * **a fault has to be reproducible.** This proxy is *deterministic*: `Delay(200ms)` delays for
+//!   200ms, `Loss { every: 2 }` drops every second request. A loss rate drawn from a generator
+//!   makes a test that fails one run in twenty, which is a test people learn to re-run instead of
+//!   read — "a fault you cannot reproduce is a rumour" is the step's whole argument;
+//! * **`kiln images` stays honest.** Every image a step declares is an image a learner has to pull
+//!   and keep. A proxy container would be a fourth one, for a fault that `std` can produce in a
+//!   hundred lines.
+//!
+//! What is deliberately *not* claimed: a container proxy can do things this cannot — inject on a
+//! real network path, cut a route, corrupt a packet at a different layer. The *classes* of failure
+//! that a delivery path has to survive are the same, and they are what this file injects: a slow
+//! answer, a connection that ends without one, a connection that never ends, and a request that
+//! never arrives. The tests judge the service's behaviour, not the injection mechanism.
+//!
+//! ## The four faults, and what each one is a stand-in for
+//!
+//! | fault | what the client sees | what it stands for in production |
+//! | --- | --- | --- |
+//! | [`Fault::Delay`] | the answer, after a wait | a subscriber that is slow, or a network that is |
+//! | [`Fault::Reset`] | the connection ends at once, with `RST` | a crashed process, a firewall, a load balancer with no backend |
+//! | [`Fault::HalfOpen`] | a connection that is accepted and then silent | a machine that is up and not answering: the failure only a deadline catches |
+//! | [`Fault::Loss`] | the request is never forwarded | a connection that goes away between two attempts |
+//!
+//! `Delay` and `Reset` are the two a retry policy is judged by; `HalfOpen` is the one that
+//! separates "has a timeout" from "has a deadline that is actually enforced"; `Loss` is what makes
+//! "nothing is lost" a statement about the *queue* rather than about one lucky attempt.
+//!
+//! ## What this file does not know
+//!
+//! Nothing about the service: no route names, no JSON, no queue. It counts connections it accepted,
+//! requests it forwarded and attempts it took, and a test reads those counters to see that the fault
+//! it asked for is the fault that happened. A proxy that injected nothing would make every test in
+//! `spec_chaos.rs` vacuously green, which is why the counter assertions are there.
+//!
+//! ## What the scaffold leaves to you
+//!
+//! The interface: a [`Fault`] to choose, a [`Proxy`] with a port of its own and three counters, and
+//! the words `Fault::as_str` answers with. Everything between them — the listener, the threads, the
+//! request framing, what each fault does to a connection, and the shared state the counters live in
+//! — is the step.
+
+#![allow(dead_code)] // a spec uses the part of this harness it needs, not all of it
+
+use std::net::SocketAddr;
+use std::sync::Arc;
+use std::time::Duration;
+
+/// One failure to inject, chosen by the test.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub enum Fault {
+    /// Forward everything: the healthy path, and how a test heals a proxy.
+    None,
+    /// Hold every request this long before forwarding it.
+    ///
+    /// The answer still arrives, so an attempt inside its deadline succeeds — which is what makes
+    /// this the fault that tests a *budget* rather than a timeout.
+    Delay(Duration),
+    /// Close the connection as soon as the request has been read, with the body still unread.
+    ///
+    /// Unread bytes in a socket's receive buffer are what makes the kernel answer with `RST`
+    /// instead of an orderly `FIN`, and an `RST` is what a client sees when the process at the
+    /// other end has died — the failure a retry policy exists for.
+    Reset,
+    /// Accept the request and then answer nothing at all, for ever.
+    ///
+    /// The request *is* delivered — this is a subscriber that went quiet, not a connection that
+    /// never arrived — and the connection stays open, so the only thing that ends it is the
+    /// client's own read deadline.
+    HalfOpen,
+    /// Forward everything except every `every`-th request, which is dropped: the connection is
+    /// closed before the request is forwarded, so the subscriber never sees it.
+    ///
+    /// Deterministic loss, on purpose: `every = 2` drops every second request, and a test that
+    /// asserts exactly how many were dropped is asserting that the injection is reproducible.
+    ///
+    /// The period counts *requests* rather than connections, and it has to: this service reuses a
+    /// connection for the attempts that go to the same target, so a connection that is only
+    /// *sometimes* dropped would take the whole reuse with it and the fault would fire once and
+    /// then hide behind a live socket. Closing the connection is still part of the fault — a
+    /// request that is dropped has to be retried on a new one.
+    Loss {
+        /// The period: every `every`-th request is dropped. `0` and `1` mean none.
+        every: usize,
+    },
+}
+
+impl Fault {
+    /// The fault as text, for a failure message a reader can act on.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn as_str(self) -> &'static str {
+        match self {
+            Self::None => "no fault",
+            Self::Delay(_) => "a delayed answer",
+            Self::Reset => "a reset connection",
+            Self::HalfOpen => "a half-open connection",
+            Self::Loss { .. } => "a dropped connection",
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+/// The state every connection thread of one proxy shares: where to forward, what to inject, and
+/// what has happened. Fill it in with what the methods below need.
+struct Shared {}
+
+/// A proxy in front of one target, with a fault the test controls.
+pub struct Proxy {
+    /// Where this proxy listens.
+    address: SocketAddr,
+    /// What the connection threads share.
+    shared: Arc<Shared>,
+}
+
